%N A399633 The city-building sequence: a(n) = the height h of the shortest building which can see exactly h earlier buildings.
%C A399633 A building a(j) "sees" an earlier building a(i) if every intermediate building a(k), i < k < j, has its top (k, a(k)) on or below the straight line joining (i, a(i)) and (j, a(j)).
%C A399633 If at some point there doesn't exist a next height h at which the condition is satisfied, the sequence is finite. The sequence has at least 2.3 billion terms.
%C A399633 If the sequence is infinite, does every number eventually appear? The earliest appearance of height h is A399763(h). The smallest unseen height is 56.
%C A399633 The apparent "spikes" at n = 16k for k = 1..7 (10, 10, 11, 10, 11, 10, 19) follow a pattern similar to the values at n for n = 2..8 (1, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 3). Similar temporary analogies seem to emerge at various scales, but do any last indefinitely?
%H A399633 Eric Fox, <a href="/A399633/b399633.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A399633 For n = 8:
%e A399633 A height of 0 would see a(6)=2 and a(7)=1, but must see 0 other buildings.
%e A399633 A height of 1 would see a(6)=2 and a(7)=1, but must see 1 other building.
%e A399633 A height of 2 would see a(4)=2, a(6)=2, and a(7)=1, but must see 2 other buildings.
%e A399633 A height of 3 sees a(4)=2, a(6)=2, and a(7)=1, exactly 3 other buildings, so a(8)=3.
%e A399633                   *
%e A399633           *   *   *
%e A399633       * * * * * * *
%e A399633 n = 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8
%Y A399633 Cf. A399763 (indices of the first appearances of each height).
